<body>
  <header class="site-header">
    <div class="header-inner">
      <div class="topline">
        <a class="wordmark" href="#top" aria-label="The Human Loop, home"><span class="wordmark-mark" aria-hidden="true">H</span><span>The Human Loop</span></a>
        <div class="header-actions">
          <nav class="main-nav" aria-label="Main navigation"><a href="#team">The team</a><a href="#future-of-se">The future</a></nav>
          <button id="theme-toggle" type="button" aria-label="Switch to night mode" title="Switch theme">☾</button>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="hero" id="top">
        <p class="eyebrow">Agentic SE Lab · University of Paderborn</p>
        <h1>People first.<br>Build what matters.</h1>
        <p class="hero-copy">We’re five computer science students asking what great software engineering looks like when AI joins the team. Different paths, shared curiosity, and plenty still worth figuring out.</p>
        <div class="hero-meta" aria-label="Team at a glance"><span class="pill">5 perspectives</span><span class="pill">Computer science</span><span class="pill">Building with intention</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </header>
  <main>
    <section id="team" aria-labelledby="team-title">
      <div class="section-heading"><div><p class="section-kicker">Meet the people</p><h2 id="team-title">The team</h2></div><p class="section-intro">From systems thinking to full-stack experiments, our perspectives are shaped by the things we build, study, and do beyond the screen.</p></div>
      <div class="member-grid">
        <article class="member-card">
          <div class="member-top"><img src="{{image:akshay.jpg}}" alt="Photo of Akshay" width="128" height="128"><div><h3>Akshay</h3><p class="member-fullname">Akshay Pradeep Kumar</p></div></div>
          <p class="study">MSc Computer Science</p>
          <p class="bio">Akshay studies for an MSc in Computer Science and enjoys running and travelling. He has recently started to love cooking, adding a new creative pursuit away from his studies. He sees AI as a way to help developers complete tasks faster and believes that people working with AI may reach new heights in technology. His outlook is that software engineering will increasingly reward creativity in deciding what to make, rather than coding alone.</p>
          <p class="interests"><strong>Interests:</strong> Running and travelling.</p><p class="fun-fact"><em>Fun fact: Akshay has started to love cooking.</em></p>
          <p class="outlook"><strong>In 5–10 years:</strong> He expects software engineering to be more about creativity in what people build than coding alone.</p>
          <p class="hot-take">🔥 We're teaching machines to code so we can finally spend our time arguing about what to build.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="member-card">
          <div class="member-top"><img src="{{image:awez.jpg}}" alt="Photo of Awez" width="128" height="128"><div><h3>Awez</h3><p class="member-fullname">Mohammed Awez</p></div></div>
          <p class="study">MSc Computer Science</p>
          <p class="bio">Mohammed Awez is studying for an MSc in Computer Science. Outside his studies, he enjoys video games and running, and he loves cooking. He sees AI as both a help and a challenge for engineers: it can take on many tasks, while also pushing people to keep learning and improving their skills. Looking ahead, he expects AI to write more code, leaving engineers with more time to decide what to build and a growing need to keep up with new technology.</p>
          <p class="interests"><strong>Interests:</strong> Video games and running.</p><p class="fun-fact"><em>Fun fact: Awez loves cooking.</em></p>
          <p class="outlook"><strong>In 5–10 years:</strong> He expects AI to do more coding while engineers focus on what to build and keep learning.</p>
          <p class="hot-take">🔥 AI is making coding easier, but being a software engineer harder!</p>
        </article>
        <article class="member-card">
          <div class="member-top"><img src="{{image:leman.png}}" alt="Photo of Leman" width="128" height="128"><div><h3>Leman</h3><p class="member-fullname">Computer science student</p></div></div>
          <p class="study">M.Sc. 5th semester, Universität Paderborn · B.Sc., Ahsanullah University of Science and Technology, Bangladesh</p>
          <p class="bio">Leman is in the fifth semester of an M.Sc. at Universität Paderborn, after completing a B.Sc. at Ahsanullah University of Science and Technology in Bangladesh. Leman is interested in data science, video games, and reading. People say Leman is a bit introverted, though Leman does not agree. In software engineering, Leman sees AI quickly handling boring setup and menial tasks, while also recognizing that it has effects on the job sector. Leman expects engineers to move from writing code line by line toward designing whole systems with AI tools.</p>
          <p class="interests"><strong>Interests:</strong> Data science, video games, and reading books.</p><p class="fun-fact"><em>Fun fact: People say Leman is a bit introverted, but Leman disagrees.</em></p>
          <p class="outlook"><strong>In 5–10 years:</strong> Leman expects more system design, with AI handling boilerplate and more of the journey from building to shipment.</p>
          <p class="hot-take">🔥 AI won't replace good software developers. Better developers are who that can use AI tools better.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="member-card">
          <div class="member-top"><img src="{{image:mazhar.png}}" alt="Photo of Mazhar" width="128" height="128"><div><h3>Mazhar</h3><p class="member-fullname">M.Sc. Computer Science student</p></div></div>
          <p class="study">First-semester M.Sc., Paderborn University · B.Sc. Computer Science and Engineering, Leading University, Bangladesh</p>
          <p class="bio">Mazhar is a first-semester M.Sc. Computer Science student at Paderborn University, with a B.Sc. in Computer Science and Engineering from Leading University in Bangladesh and three and a half years of software experience. He is interested in building products, automating repetitive tasks, and learning system architecture, networking, and security. He sees AI making routine coding cheaper and putting more emphasis on architecture, reliability, and security. After returning to university, he is learning to delegate to AI while wondering whether it will leave him a job. <a class="portfolio-link" href="https://cloud-007.github.io/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Visit Mazhar’s portfolio</a>.</p>
          <p class="interests"><strong>Interests:</strong> Product building, automation, engineering fundamentals, and exchanging ideas.</p><p class="fun-fact"><em>Fun fact: After three and a half years in software, Mazhar is back at university learning to delegate to AI—and hoping it still leaves him a job.</em></p>
          <p class="outlook"><strong>In 5–10 years:</strong> Mazhar is unsure what lies ahead and hopes safety, guidance, security, and responsible use keep pace with AI’s progress.</p>
          <p class="hot-take">🔥 AI puts building within more people's reach; curiosity, creativity and learning how to guide it make the difference.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="member-card">
          <div class="member-top"><img src="{{image:sachin.jpg}}" alt="Photo of Sachin" width="128" height="128"><div><h3>Sachin</h3><p class="member-fullname">Computer science student</p></div></div>
          <p class="study">First-semester M.Sc. Computer Science, University of Paderborn · B.E. Computer Science and Engineering, DSCE, Bangalore</p>
          <p class="bio">Sachin is a first-semester M.Sc. Computer Science student at the University of Paderborn, with a B.E. in Computer Science and Engineering from DSCE in Bangalore. He is interested in AI, full-stack application development, fitness, cooking, and competitive gaming. He likes AI for setup and standard testing scripts, but warns that generated code can hide logic vulnerabilities and should not be trusted blindly. In five to ten years, he expects engineers to focus on specifications, boundaries, and validation as software architects and judges, rather than typists.</p>
          <p class="interests"><strong>Interests:</strong> AI, full-stack development, fitness, cooking, and competitive gaming.</p><p class="fun-fact"><em>Fun fact: Sachin once spent three hours debugging a production issue caused by a misspelling of his own first name in a configuration string.</em></p>
          <p class="outlook"><strong>In 5–10 years:</strong> Sachin expects a shift toward specification, prompt containment, and automated validation over manually writing raw code.</p>
          <p class="hot-take">🔥 AI shouldn't replace our critical thinking; it should augment it. Outsourcing our logic to algorithms breeds intellectual laziness.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section id="future-of-se" aria-labelledby="future-title">
      <div class="future-section">
        <div class="section-heading"><p class="section-kicker">A shared question</p><h2 id="future-title">The Future of Software Engineering</h2><p class="section-intro">We don’t have one prediction, but we share a sense of where the work is moving: less repetitive implementation, more thoughtful direction.</p></div>
        <div class="future-grid">
          <article class="future-point"><h3>From typing to designing</h3><p>As AI takes on boilerplate, setup, tests, and more routine coding, engineers can spend more time shaping systems and deciding what is worth building.</p></article>
          <article class="future-point"><h3>Human judgment matters more</h3><p>Clear specifications, creative problem framing, critical thinking, and good decisions will matter as much as the ability to produce code.</p></article>
          <article class="future-point"><h3>Fundamentals are the safety net</h3><p>AI-generated code can conceal security flaws and unreliable assumptions. Strong engineering fundamentals help teams question, validate, and secure what they ship.</p></article>
          <article class="future-point"><h3>Progress needs responsibility</h3><p>More capable tools bring uncertainty about jobs and everyday life. Guidance and responsible use deserve attention alongside new capabilities.</p></article>
        </div>
        <p class="future-conclusion">Our shared bet: the best engineers won’t simply ask AI to build faster. They’ll know what to build, guide the tools well, and take responsibility for the result.</p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
  <footer><div class="footer-inner"><p>Built by an agent, supervised by humans. · 2026 · The Human Loop</p></div></footer>
  <script>
    (() => {
      const button = document.getElementById("theme-toggle");
      const root = document.documentElement;
      function updateButton() {
        const dark = root.getAttribute("data-theme") === "dark";
        button.textContent = dark ? "☀" : "☾";
        button.setAttribute("aria-label", dark ? "Switch to day mode" : "Switch to night mode");
        button.title = dark ? "Switch to day mode" : "Switch to night mode";
      }
      updateButton();
      button.addEventListener("click", () => {
        const theme = root.getAttribute("data-theme") === "dark" ? "light" : "dark";
        root.setAttribute("data-theme", theme);
        try { localStorage.setItem("upb-theme", theme); } catch (_) {}
        updateButton();
      });
    })();
  </script>
</body>
</html>"}}

