    </section>

    <section id="perspectives" aria-labelledby="perspectives-title">
      <div class="comparison">
        <div class="section-heading">
          <p class="section-kicker">Same questions, different answers</p>
          <h2 id="perspectives-title">Where we agree—and where we don’t</h2>
          <p class="section-intro">Our profiles point to some common ground, but not a single, settled prediction about AI or the jobs around it.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="comparison-grid">
          <article class="comparison-point">
            <h3>Common ground: less busywork</h3>
            <p>All five see AI helping with routine tasks—from faster task completion and coding to setup, boilerplate, and standard tests. That creates room for people to focus on what a product should do and how its systems fit together.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="comparison-point">
            <h3>Common ground: people still need judgment</h3>
            <p>The team’s views emphasize different human strengths: creativity, learning, system design, fundamentals, critical thinking, and checking reliability and security. Using AI well still calls for responsibility.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="comparison-point">
            <h3>Different outlooks on how far automation goes</h3>
            <p>Some expect increasingly creative work, AI-written code, or system-level automation. Sachin predicts raw-code typing becoming a legacy workflow. Mazhar is less certain about what the next five to ten years will bring.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="comparison-point">
            <h3>Different levels of confidence about the impact</h3>
            <p>Akshay sees potential for people to reach new heights; Awez highlights pressure to keep learning. Leman notes effects on the job sector, while Mazhar worries progress could outpace safe adoption and change people’s roles.</p>
          </article>
        </div>
        <p class="comparison-note">So we agree that engineering is changing; we’re still debating how quickly, how far, and whether society will be ready.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="future-of-se" aria-labelledby="future-title">
      <div class="future-panel">
        <div class="section-heading">
          <p class="section-kicker">A shared question</p>
          <h2 id="future-title">The Future of Software Engineering</h2>
          <p class="section-intro">Together, our perspectives suggest a shift away from repetitive implementation and toward thoughtful direction—without pretending the outcome is certain.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="future-grid">
          <article class="future-point">
            <h3>From typing to designing</h3>
            <p>As AI takes on boilerplate, setup, tests, and more routine coding, engineers can spend more time shaping systems and deciding what is worth building.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="future-point">
            <h3>Human judgment matters</h3>
            <p>Creative problem framing, clear specifications, critical thinking, and sound decisions will matter alongside the ability to produce code.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="future-point">
            <h3>Fundamentals are the safety net</h3>
            <p>Generated code can conceal vulnerabilities and unreliable assumptions. Engineering fundamentals help teams question, validate, and secure what they ship.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="future-point">
            <h3>Progress needs responsibility</h3>
            <p>New capabilities bring uncertainty about jobs and everyday life. Guidance and responsible use deserve attention alongside AI’s continued development.</p>
          </article>
        </div>
        <p class="future-conclusion">Our shared bet: the strongest engineers will know what to build, guide AI tools thoughtfully, and take responsibility for the result.</p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>

  <footer>
    <div class="footer-shell">
      <p>Built by an agent, supervised by humans. · 2026 · The Human Loop</p>
    </div>
