        <div class="section-heading">
          <p class="section-kicker">Same questions, different answers</p>
          <h2 id="perspectives-title">Where we agree—and where we don’t</h2>
          <p class="section-intro">Our profiles point to some common ground, but not a single, settled prediction about AI or the jobs around it.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="comparison-grid">
          <article class="comparison-point">
            <h3>Common ground: less busywork</h3>
            <p>All five see AI helping with routine tasks—from faster task completion and coding to setup, boilerplate, and standard tests. That creates room for people to focus on what a product should do and how its systems fit together.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="comparison-point">
            <h3>Common ground: people still need judgment</h3>
            <p>The team’s views emphasize different human strengths: creativity, learning, system design, fundamentals, critical thinking, and checking reliability and security. Using AI well still calls for responsibility.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="comparison-point">
            <h3>Different outlooks on how far automation goes</h3>
            <p>Some expect increasingly creative work, AI-written code, or system-level automation. Sachin predicts raw-code typing becoming a legacy workflow. Mazhar is less certain about what the next five to ten years will bring.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="comparison-point">
            <h3>Different levels of confidence about the impact</h3>
            <p>Akshay sees potential for people to reach new heights; Awez highlights pressure to keep learning. Leman notes effects on the job sector, while Mazhar worries progress could outpace safe adoption and change people’s roles.</p>
          </article>
        </div>
        <p class="comparison-note">So we agree that engineering is changing; we’re still debating how quickly, how far, and whether society will be ready.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

